---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footprint from "../../components/Footprint.astro";
import { OUTCOMES, meetings, stats } from "../../lib/data";

const narrative = meetings.filter((m) => !m.structured);
---
<Base title="About">
  <section class="prose" style="padding: 2.5rem 0 0">
    <p class="eyebrow">Method, sources, and limits</p>
    <h1 style="font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.8rem); margin:.4rem 0 1rem">About this index</h1>

    <p>
      The City of Kingston publishes Historic Landmarks Preservation Commission
      minutes as PDFs, reachable only through a dropdown archive at
      <a href="https://kingston-ny.gov/Agendas">kingston-ny.gov/Agendas</a>.
      There is no list you can scan, no search, and no way to ask what the
      commission has said about a given house. This site is that list.
    </p>

    <p>
      {stats.meetings} meetings from {stats.firstDate} to {stats.lastDate} were
      downloaded, read, and split into {stats.items} agenda items carrying
      {stats.decisions} recorded decisions. Nothing here is summarised by hand —
      every outcome is derived from the commission's own wording, and every item
      links back to the PDF it came from.
    </p>

    <h2>Two commissions, not one</h2>
    <p>
      The HLPC and the Heritage Area Commission are separate bodies with
      different jurisdictions. The HLPC issues Certificates of Appropriateness
      for the five local historic districts under Form-Based Code §405.26.L; the
      HAC reviews the wider state Heritage Area. Between January 2021 and August
      2023 they met together and voted separately on the same applications, so a
      board is recorded on each decision rather than on the meeting. Counting
      them together would double every outcome and credit HLPC approvals to the
      wrong body.
    </p>

    <h2>The key</h2>
    <p>
      Outcome colours follow the Sanborn fire-insurance map key that surveyors
      used to record what a building was made of — blue for stone, yellow for
      frame, red for brick, grey for iron. A settled approval is stone; anything
      still provisional is frame; a refusal is brick; procedural business is iron.
    </p>
    <ul class="key" style="margin: 0 0 1.25rem">
      {Object.entries(OUTCOMES).map(([outcome, meta]) => (
        <li><button type="button" tabindex="-1" style="cursor:default"><Footprint outcome={outcome} />{meta.label}</button></li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>What this does not cover</h2>
    <p>
      Coverage starts in 2020. Minutes back to 2010 exist, but they are scans
      with poor OCR or loose narrative, and machine-reading them would invent
      precision the documents do not have.
    </p>
    <p>
      {narrative.length} meetings between January 2020 and June 2021 predate the
      commission's <span class="mono">DECISION:</span> convention. Their items are
      indexed and their text is searchable, but their outcomes are not yet
      classified — they are marked on each meeting page rather than guessed at.
    </p>
    <p>
      Votes are kept as the minutes wrote them. The commission records bare
      initials that only resolve against that meeting's roster, and the notation
      changed several times between 2020 and 2026, so expanding them would be a
      guess dressed as data.
    </p>

    <h2>Use it as an index, not a record</h2>
    <p>
      This is unofficial. The signed minutes on the city's site are the record.
      If something here matters to a decision you are making, follow the link and
      read the PDF.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
